<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Vickery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Vickery was an American historian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. He wrote widely on early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> history, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> period in Cambodia 1975–1982, and on Thai history. In the late 1980s he was among the scholars who questioned the authenticity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> inscription, the founding text of Sukhothai history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chenla, or Zhenla, is the name the Chinese gave to an entity that sent tribute to their emperors; neither it nor Funan is known in Old Khmer, and <b>Michael Vickery</b> holds the original meanings of both unknown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people's ethnicity is debated: <b>Michael Vickery</b> holds that on present evidence they can only have been Khmer, Vietnamese scholars such as Mac Duong think them Austronesian, and the only writings of the period are Sanskrit inscriptions in Pallava Grantha script, which say nothing of the vernacular.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 1980s scholars including <b>Michael Vickery</b> and Piriya Krairiksh questioned whether the inscription was a later composition, possibly of the nineteenth century, and the debate remains unresolved, though many specialists accept it as substantially of the period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
